age and moving forward together or  are you going to have debates over what  we teach in schools uh and many ways you  know textbooks have have actually have a  long history of being debated whether  they recognize the contributions of  minorities over history uh how you treat  um historic inequities and and perhaps  the inconvenient truths of of the  country's history um that's quite common  and then you know with regard to census  questioning that's a matter of power uh  when you are excluding you know people  from a population count you're also  excluding them from power and so these  questions are integral and they're  produced by the fraught politics of  demographic change can you give some  other examples of the the sort of uh  battles that are being had so we've got  the citizenship question critical race  theory you've talked about textbooks and  how history is uh taught there's let's  make english officially the one official  language of the united states what are  some of the other kind of uh uh ways in  which we can see this battle thought  sure you can see it in the battles over  culture  over music and over art and over uh  sports  i think back to khan kaepernick's  effective cancellation from the nfl  when he was taking a knee before the  national anthem in protest of police  brutality and discrimination i think of  the battles over confederate monuments  and the desire to protect them right um  you can also look at the world of  commerce you know do we see racialized  poverty or do we see the the marketplace  as a as a place of interdependency  the politics of affirmative action in in  whether it's  education or employment  all of these are very much related to  demographic change and how we manage it  so there's the question there's sort of  empirical questions about is the  demographic change happening and what  data points do we have as you've  researched in the book about other  places that that it's happened that's  one side of it there's also th